STANCES = [
("Stance 160 - Request-length heterogeneity in the live traffic mix, not the parallel axis, drives queueing delay, so a layout chosen on a synthetic uniform-length trace will not survive production.",
 "Production prompt and generation lengths are heavy-tailed, and the two layouts absorb that tail differently: a tensor layout keeps every GPU on the same step boundary so a single very long generation extends the step for the whole batch, whereas a pipeline layout lets stages run different micro-batches so a long generation occupies one stage's slot rather than the entire step. Choosing on a uniform-length synthetic trace therefore measures a workload that does not exist. Falsifiable hypothesis H160: replaying a length-matched production trace instead of a uniform-length synthetic trace will change the p99 gap between the layouts by more than it changes the p50 gap, and may invert the p99 ordering while leaving the p50 ordering intact (ESTIMATE; derivation: head-of-line blocking cost scales with the variance of per-request service time, which affects tail quantiles far more than the median, so a change in the length distribution moves p99 first). Controlled experiment: build one trace by sampling arrival times and (prompt_len, output_len) pairs jointly from a production log window, replay the identical trace file against both arms in closed loop at matched concurrency, and report p50/p95/p99 separately for TTFT and TPOT plus the queue-wait component. Rollback gate: if a layout wins on the synthetic trace but loses p99 on the replayed production trace by more than the SLO margin, the synthetic result is discarded and the layout is not promoted."),
("Stance 161 - Failure and recovery semantics differ between the axes, so availability, not steady-state latency, should decide when a single GPU can fail.",
 "The two layouts have different blast radii. Under tensor parallelism every rank participates in every collective, so the loss of one GPU stalls the whole replica and the collective communicator must be torn down and rebuilt; under pipeline parallelism the loss of one stage breaks the chain equally but the surviving stages hold distinct weight shards, which changes what must be reloaded. Either way a replica is lost, but the recovery time and the amount of state re-read from storage differ. Falsifiable hypothesis H161: mean time to return to SLO after a single-GPU fault differs between the layouts by more than the steady-state latency difference between them, making availability, not latency, the dominant term in the decision (ESTIMATE; derivation: recovery time is dominated by checkpoint or weight re-read plus communicator re-initialisation, both of which are seconds-scale, while the steady-state latency difference is milliseconds-scale, so the two terms differ by orders of magnitude). Controlled experiment: inject a deterministic single-rank fault, using device reset or process kill at a fixed point in the request stream, at least five times per arm, and measure time from fault to first request again meeting the SLO, plus the count of requests failed versus requests retried. Rollback gate: a layout whose measured recovery exceeds the error budget for the expected fault rate is rejected even if it wins every latency benchmark."),
("Stance 162 - Collective algorithm selection and topology detection inside the communication library are a hidden variable that must be pinned before either layout can be compared.",
 "NCCL chooses its algorithm and protocol at runtime from detected topology, message size and environment, so the same tensor-parallel configuration can execute a ring or a tree all-reduce, over NVLink or over PCIe, on two nodes that look identical in an inventory. If the arms are not pinned, the experiment silently compares algorithm choices rather than parallel layouts. Falsifiable hypothesis H162: pinning the collective algorithm, protocol and detected topology across arms will reduce run-to-run variance in the tensor arm substantially more than in the pipeline arm, because the pipeline arm's point-to-point sends have far less algorithmic freedom to vary (ESTIMATE; derivation: the pipeline path uses simple point-to-point transfers with essentially one implementation, while all-reduce has multiple algorithm and protocol combinations selectable per message size). Controlled experiment: capture the communication library's debug and topology dump for both arms, assert byte-for-byte identical topology detection, then repeat the latency measurement with the algorithm and protocol explicitly pinned and again with automatic selection, reporting variance in both conditions. Rollback gate: if pinning is required to reproduce the winning result, the pinned settings become part of the deployed configuration and are version-controlled with the layout; an unpinned deployment does not inherit the benchmark's conclusion."),
("Stance 163 - KV-cache capacity, not compute, is usually the binding constraint at the latency SLO, so the axes must be compared at equal admissible concurrency rather than at equal batch size.",
 "Tensor parallelism shards attention heads and therefore divides per-GPU KV bytes by the tensor degree, raising the number of concurrent sequences the replica can hold; pipeline parallelism gives each stage the full KV for its own layers only, which also reduces per-GPU KV but leaves per-stage capacity coupled to the layer split. Comparing at a fixed batch size hides this: the layout with more cache headroom can serve the same batch without preemption while the other silently evicts and recomputes. Falsifiable hypothesis H163: at the concurrency where the SLO is first violated, the two layouts differ more in preemption and recompute counters than in per-step compute time, showing that the binding constraint is cache capacity rather than arithmetic throughput (ESTIMATE; derivation: decode is memory-bandwidth and capacity bound rather than FLOP bound, so the first resource to saturate as concurrency rises is cache residency). Controlled experiment: sweep closed-loop concurrency upward in fixed steps on both arms until the SLO breaks, and at each step record free cache blocks, preemption count, recomputed tokens and step time, rather than reporting a single throughput number. Rollback gate: a layout is not promoted if it meets the SLO only at a concurrency that leaves less than the declared cache headroom, because normal traffic drift would then push it into preemption."),
("Stance 164 - Quantization and weight layout interact with the tensor degree, so a layout comparison run at a different numerical precision per arm is not a layout comparison at all.",
 "Weight-only and activation quantization schemes have group or block structures that must divide cleanly across the sharded dimension; a tensor degree that splits a quantization group forces either a fallback kernel or a re-quantization, changing both speed and numerics. Pipeline parallelism does not touch the sharded dimension in the same way, because it splits by layer, so the same checkpoint can behave differently under the two axes even with identical settings on paper. Falsifiable hypothesis H164: for a fixed quantized checkpoint, increasing the tensor degree past the point where the quantization group no longer divides the sharded dimension will produce a discontinuous jump in per-step latency or an observable change in output distribution, while the pipeline arm shows no such discontinuity (ESTIMATE; derivation: group-quantized kernels have alignment requirements on the reduced dimension; violating them triggers a slower general path or a layout conversion, which is a step change rather than a smooth trend). Controlled experiment: hold the checkpoint, engine version and batching policy fixed, sweep only the tensor degree, and at each point record both latency and a numerical parity metric against the unsharded reference; then repeat the sweep on the pipeline axis. Rollback gate: any arm whose numerical parity metric falls outside the pre-declared tolerance is disqualified before its latency is considered."),
("Stance 165 - Host-side overhead, including Python dispatch, sampling and detokenisation, can exceed the collective cost at small batch sizes, so the measured difference may not be on the device at all.",
 "At the low concurrency that latency-sensitive services actually run, per-step device work is small and the fixed per-step host cost, launch overhead, sampler, stop-string checking and detokenisation, becomes a comparable share. A tensor layout adds device-side collectives but keeps one logical step; a pipeline layout adds more scheduling decisions and cross-stage handoffs on the host. Attributing the whole difference to the parallel axis without splitting host from device is therefore unsafe. Falsifiable hypothesis H165: at concurrency one, host-side time is a larger fraction of inter-token latency than collective time in the tensor arm, and enabling graph capture to remove launch overhead compresses the gap between the arms rather than preserving it (ESTIMATE; derivation: decode kernels at batch one are short, so fixed per-launch and per-step host costs occupy a proportionally large share, and graph capture removes per-launch cost without changing collective payloads). Controlled experiment: profile both arms with a device timeline plus host-side sampling profiler at concurrency 1, 4 and 16, report the host and device split of each step, then repeat with graph capture enabled and disabled. Rollback gate: if the layout advantage disappears once host overhead is equalised, the layout change is not adopted and the work is redirected to the host path, which is the cheaper fix."),
("Stance 166 - Weight and checkpoint load topology differ between the axes, so deployment agility, meaning how fast a replica can be started or rescheduled, is part of the decision.",
 "A tensor layout requires every rank to read a shard of every layer, producing many small correlated reads spread across the whole checkpoint; a pipeline layout has each stage read a contiguous layer range, producing fewer, larger, more sequential reads. On networked or object storage these access patterns have materially different behaviour, which shows up as replica start time and therefore as how quickly capacity can be added during a traffic spike. Falsifiable hypothesis H166: replica cold-start time differs between the layouts by a factor larger than their steady-state latency ratio, and the difference is larger on networked storage than on local NVMe, because the access pattern rather than the byte volume drives it (ESTIMATE; derivation: total bytes read is roughly the model size in both cases, so any difference must come from request size and concurrency of the reads, which is precisely what networked storage penalises). Controlled experiment: measure time from process start to first token served for both arms, five repeats each, on local NVMe and on the production storage backend, with the page cache dropped before each run, and record read size histograms. Rollback gate: if the layout cannot meet the autoscaling start-time budget on the production storage backend, it is rejected regardless of steady-state latency, since the service would then fail to absorb spikes."),
("Stance 167 - The decision is only valid for a declared traffic operating point, so the artifact that ships must be a bounded envelope, not a single winning configuration.",
 "A layout comparison produces a result conditional on model, fabric, concurrency, length distribution and engine version. Shipping the winner as an unconditional verdict discards those conditions and guarantees a future incident when one of them drifts. The defensible artifact is an envelope: the range of concurrency and length distribution within which the measured ordering held, with the conditions written down as assertable checks. Falsifiable hypothesis H167: the measured ordering of the two layouts is not monotone across the concurrency sweep, so there exists at least one crossover point inside the plausible operating range, which means a single winner cannot be correct everywhere (ESTIMATE; derivation: the pipeline bubble fraction shrinks as in-flight micro-batches increase while the tensor collective cost is roughly concurrency-independent per step, so the two cost curves have different slopes and generically cross). Controlled experiment: sweep concurrency across the full plausible range on both arms, locate the crossover if one exists, and express the result as an interval with the ordering annotated per interval rather than as a single number. Rollback gate: a monitor asserts the live operating point stays inside the validated envelope; leaving the envelope triggers re-measurement, and a layout with no validated envelope is not promoted."),
("Stance 168 - Cross-node placement and fabric asymmetry mean the layout decision is really a topology decision, and an experiment that ignores where the ranks landed is unreproducible.",
 "Once more than one node is involved, the tensor group must not straddle a node boundary if the collective is on the decode critical path, because the inter-node fabric latency floor is far above the intra-node one; the pipeline boundary is the correct place to cross nodes, since it carries only small point-to-point hidden-state transfers. A scheduler that places ranks without topology awareness can therefore produce two runs of the identical configuration with different performance. Falsifiable hypothesis H168: two runs of the same configuration differing only in whether the tensor group straddles a node boundary will differ in inter-token latency by more than the difference between the two layouts measured within a node, making placement the larger effect (ESTIMATE; derivation: intra-node NVLink and inter-node RDMA per-hop latency differ by roughly an order of magnitude for small latency-bound messages, whereas the layout difference is a modest multiple of one such hop). Controlled experiment: record the resolved rank-to-device and rank-to-node mapping, plus the fabric interface used per rank, as a required artifact of every run; deliberately run one arm with the tensor group split across nodes and one with it contained, holding everything else fixed. Rollback gate: any result whose placement artifact is missing is void; a deployment whose scheduler cannot guarantee topology-aware placement does not inherit the intra-node conclusion."),
("Stance 169 - Disaggregating prefill from decode changes the question, because the two phases have opposite parallelism preferences and should not be forced onto one layout.",
 "Prefill is compute-bound and highly parallel across the sequence, so it tolerates and benefits from wide sharding; decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and latency-sensitive, so it is punished by every synchronous collective. Serving both phases from a single layout forces one compromise for two different cost structures. A disaggregated design, prefill workers and decode workers with the KV cache transferred between them, lets each phase pick its own axis, at the cost of a cache transfer on the critical path for TTFT. Falsifiable hypothesis H169: for a given SLO, a disaggregated configuration with per-phase layouts meets the TPOT target at higher concurrency than any single-layout configuration, provided the KV transfer time stays below the TTFT slack, and it fails to do so once the transfer exceeds that slack (ESTIMATE; derivation: the transfer volume is the prefilled KV for the request, which grows with prompt length, so the design has a prompt-length threshold above which the transfer no longer fits in the TTFT budget). Controlled experiment: measure the three arms, tensor-only, pipeline-only and disaggregated, on the same replayed trace at matched total GPU count, and instrument KV transfer bytes and time per request separately from prefill and decode compute. Rollback gate: if measured KV transfer time exceeds the declared TTFT slack at the p95 prompt length, disaggregation is rejected for this workload and the single-layout result stands."),
]

